{title: Thy Word Is Like a Garden, Lord}
{artist: Edwin Hodder / Gottfried W. Fink}
{genre: Hymn}
{style: Traditional}
{flow: Meditative}
{key: A}
{key_original: Bb}
{tempo: 110}
{time: 4/4}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Cyber Hymnal score (The Book of Hymns (Nashville, Tennessee: United Methodist Publishing House, 1966), # 343)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[E]Thy [A]Word is [E]like [D]a [A]garden, Lord,
[A]With [D]flow[Bm]ers [E7]bright and [A]fair;
[A]And everyone who seeks may pluck
[A]A [E]lovely [B7]cluster [E]there.
[E]Thy Word is [A]like a [E7]deep, deep [A]mine,
[A]And [Bm]jewels rich [B7]and [E]rare
[E]Are [A]hidden [Bm]in its [A]mighty depths
[A]For [Bm]every [A]searcher there.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[E]Thy [A]Word is [E]like [D]a [A]starry host:
[A]A [D]thou[Bm]sand [E7]rays of [A]light
[A]Are seen to guide the traveler
[A]And [E]make his [B7]pathway [E]bright.
[E]Thy Word is [A]like an [E7]armo[A]ry
[A]Where [Bm]soldiers may [B7]re[E]pair,
[E]And [A]find, for [Bm]life’s long [A]battle day,
[A]All [Bm]needful [A]weapons there.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[E]O [A]may I [E]love [D]Thy [A]precious Word,
[A]May [D]I [Bm]ex[E7]plore the [A]mine,
[A]May I its fragrant flowers glean,
[A]May [E]light up[B7]on me [E]shine!
[E]O may I [A]find my [E7]armor [A]there!
[A]Thy [Bm]Word my trus[B7]ty [E]sword,
[E]I’ll [A]learn to [Bm]fight with [A]every foe
[A]The [Bm]battle [A]of the Lord.
{end_of_verse}
